[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-4-3-1",
    "slot_key": "Processing behavior when data are incomplete or conflicting",
    "slot_value": "Use a clear source of truth for each concept and map equivalent terms back to that shared definition. Preserve the original source wording, but normalize it for operations and reporting. When data are genuinely conflicting or ambiguous, flag it rather than guessing silently, and show confidence or provenance so operators can assess reliability. When the data are incomplete, need validation, or the decision has higher operational or policy impact, rely on existing reports or manual review rather than automation. Escalate from general awareness to a recommended action only when the connected-vehicle insight has strong corroboration from another source (traffic sensors, weather data, or a field report), crosses an operational threshold, and is clear enough in impact/urgency to justify a recommendation. Use connected-vehicle data to support a recommendation when it shows a broader or earlier pattern than fixed sensors, and that pattern is consistent over a short period rather than a single spike. Prefer the fixed-sensor view when it is clearly available, stable, and directly measuring the condition of interest; CV insight should supplement or accelerate the decision, not override a solid sensor-based picture unless there is a clear reason to do so. Also verify outputs for major construction changes, special events, unusual traffic patterns where historical assumptions stop working, multi-source conflicts, incomplete data, long gaps in timestamps, and results based on model estimates rather than direct observation; when the consequence of acting on a bad output is high, verify first. If the output will drive an operational decision with safety, congestion, or public messaging impact, and any warning conditions are present, verify immediately before action. Even a confident-looking output should be verified if it depends on older data, a single source, or a model estimate during a fast-changing situation. Verify whenever the result seems unusually clean compared with surrounding conditions, because that can hide a bad feed or a stale update; confidence alone is not enough if the context suggests the answer could have changed since the last update. If the prototype can’t keep the consolidated picture current enough to trust, or if one of the core feeds is stale or missing long enough to affect decisions, trigger fallback to the existing workflow. Treat it as degraded when users would have to second-guess whether the information is complete or current, or when delay or missing data starts changing the operational answer; the output must remain reliable enough for action without extra manual checking.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

