y, congestion, or public messaging impact and warning conditions are present; even confident-looking outputs must be verified if they rely on older data, a single source, or a model estimate in a fast-changing situation, or if they look unusually clean compared with surrounding conditions because that can indicate a bad feed or stale update. Users should stay in the existing process whenever the task is routine, local, or already well covered by a current operational system. They should switch to the consolidated view when they need to compare multiple feeds, resolve conflicting information, or make a cross-functional decision that spans safety, traffic, weather, or traveler impacts. A practical handoff point is when the user would otherwise have to check two or more systems manually to answer the question. If the prototype can show a clearer combined picture faster, that’s when it should take over; if not, the current process should remain the primary workflow. The prototype is considered degraded enough to trigger fallback when it can’t keep the consolidated picture current enough to trust, when a core feed is stale or missing long enough to affect decisions, or when users would have to second-guess whether the information is complete or current. A minor delay may be tolerable, but once delay or missing data starts changing the operational answer, users should be pushed back to the existing workflow; the key threshold is whether the output is still reliable enough for action without extra manual checking. As a practical rule, if a source can influence a real-time decision, then it needs to meet the same access and quality expectations as the rest of the operational system. If it can’t meet those expectations, it stays in the demo or validation lane and does not flow into routine workflows.",
